(D1 — Discrepancy 1 repro: a global declared BELOW a function is read
 inside that function as a raw machine word unless it is a `number`.
 LANGUAGE.md:705 says top-level variables "are global and can be used
 inside functions" and puts no ordering condition on that. The same five
 globals, read at top level, all print correctly; read from a function
 defined above them, only the number survives. The text and buffer
 addresses are static rodata/heap addresses and the float is its IEEE-754
 bit pattern, so every wrong answer looks like data. The list and buffer
 addresses vary per run, so this probe is compared on its EXIT CODE:
 it exits 3 because the in-function read of `label` did not equal the
 top-level one.
  Ran (values seen on 2026-08-21): in-function reads printed
    42 / 4210888 / 4612811918334230528 / 140547700088832 / 140547700080640
  against top-level 42 / hello / 2.5 / [1, 2, 3] / ABC.
 Ran: VOX_CORE_PATH=/home/josj/scr/english/vox/coreasm /home/josj/scr/english/vox/target/release/vox D1.vox -o p && ./p
 expected output:
   42
   4210888
   4612811918334230528
   139733748330496
   139733748322304
   --- the same globals, read at top level ---
   42
   hello
   2.5
   [1, 2, 3]
   ABC
   exit 3
)
To 'show all'.
  Print counter.
  Print label.
  Print ratio.
  Print items.
  Print payload.

a number called counter is 42.
a text called label is "hello".
a float called ratio is 2.5.
a list called items is [1, 2, 3].
a buffer called payload is "ABC".

'show all'.
Print "--- the same globals, read at top level ---".
Print counter.
Print label.
Print ratio.
Print items.
Print payload.
Exit 3.
